many insects, especially of ''Coleoptera;'' the elytrum. {{smaller|In hemipterous insects the wing-cases are technically called ''hemielytra.'' Wing-cases are always the modified fore wings; when these wings are but little modified, as in orthopterous insects, they are called ''tegmina.'' See cuts under ''beetle, chrysalis, clavus, Coleoptera,'' and ''katydid.'' Also ''wing-cover.''}}

'''wing-cell''' (wing′sel), ''n.'' In ''entom.'', any one of the spaces between the nerves or veins of the wing. See cuts under ''nervure, venation,'' and ''wing.''—'''Didymous, petiolate, radiated wing-cells.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}

'''wing-compass''' (wing′kum″pas), ''n.'' A compass with an arc-shaped piece which passes through the opposite leg, and is clamped by a set-screw.

'''wing-conch''' (wing′kongk), ''n.'' A wing-shell.

'''wing-cover''' (wing′kuv″ėr), ''n.'' In ''entom.'', same as ''wing-case.''—'''Mutilated wing-covers.''' {{smaller|See ''mutilated.''}}

'''wing-covert''' (wing′kuv″ėrt), ''n.'' In ''ornith.'', any one of the small feathers which overlie or underlie the flight-feathers of the wing; a covert of the wing. See ''covert, n.'', 6 (with cut), ''tectrices,'' and first cut under ''wing.''—'''Under wing-coverts.''' {{smaller|See ''under.''}}

'''winged''' (wingd or wing′ed), ''a.'' [< ME. ''winged, wenged;'' < ''wing'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' Having or wearing wings, in any sense: as, the ''winged'' horse (Pegasus); the ''winged'' god (Mercury); a ''winged'' (feathered) arrow; a ''winged'' ship.
{{smaller block|<poem>Steer hither, steer your ''winged'' pines,
All beaten mariners.</poem>
{{right|''W. Browne'', Syrens’ Song.}}
There is also a little contemptible ''winged'' creature, an inhabitant of my aerial element.
{{right|''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, p. 28.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
'''2.''' In ''her.'', having wings. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') Noting a bird when the wings are of a different tincture from the body. [Rare.] (''b'') Noting an object not usually having wings: as, a ''winged'' column.}} '''3.''' In ''bot.'', ''anat.'', and ''conch.'', alate; alated; having a part resembling or likened to a wing: as, a ''winged'' shell or bone; a ''winged'' seed. See cuts under ''sphenoid'', ''wing-shell'', and ''wing'', ''n.'', 9 (''c'').—'''4.''' Abounding with wings, and hence with birds; swarming with birds. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''wing’d'' air dark’d with plumes.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 730.}}}}
'''5.''' Moving or passing on or as on wings; swift; rapid.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ther mighte I seen
''Wenged'' wondres faste fleen.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 2118.}}
<poem>Come, Tamburlaine! now whet thy ''winged'' sword.</poem>
{{right|''Marlowe'', Tamburlaine, I., ii. 3.}}
<poem>With Fear oppress’d,
In ''winged'' Words he thus the Queen address’d.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', Hymn to Venus.}}}}
'''6.''' Soaring; lofty; elevated; sublime.
{{smaller block|How ''winged'' the sentiment that virtue is to be followed for its own sake, because its essence is divine!
{{right|''J. S. Harford'', Michael Angelo, v.}}
He [Emerson] looked far away over the heads of his hearers, with a vague kind of expectation, as into some private heaven of invention, and the ''winged'' period came at last obedient to his spell. {{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 383.}}}}
'''7.''' Disabled in the wing; having the wing broken.
{{smaller block|You will often recover ''winged'' birds as full of life as before the bone was broken. {{right|''Coues'', Key to N. A. Birds, p. 16.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''winglet''' (wing′let), ''n.'' [< ''wing'' + ''-let.''] A little wing. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''ornith.'', the bastard wing, or alula. (''b'') In ''entom.'': (1) The alula, a membrane under the base of the elytra of many ''Coleoptera.''}}
{{smaller block|When he took off the ''winglets'', either wholly or partially, the buzzing ceased.
{{right|''Kirby and Spence'', Entomology, II. 306.}}}}
{{smaller|(2) The pterygium, a lateral expansion on each side of the end of the rostrum, found in many weevils.}}

'''wing-membrane''' (wing′mem″brān), ''n.'' The skin of the wing of a bat; the alar membrane.

'''wing-nervure''' (wing′nėr″vụ̄r), ''n.'' In ''entom.'', a nervure (which see, with cut).—{{smaller|'''Uncinate wing-nervures.''' See ''uncinate.''}}

'''wing-net''' (wing′net), ''n.'' A winged kind of stake-net, used in the St. Lawrence salmon-fishery.

'''wing-pad''' (wing′pad), ''n.'' One of the undeveloped, pad-like wings of an active pupa, as of a young grasshopper. See cut under ''Caloptenus.''

'''wing-passage''' (wing′pas″āj), ''n.'' ''Naut.'', a passage along the sides of a ship in the hold. ''Thearle'', Naval Arch., ¶ 154.

'''wing-pen''' (wing′pen), ''n.'' An inclosure for salt or ice in the hold of a vessel.

'''wing-post''' (wing′pōst), ''n.'' A post or messenger which travels on the wing; a carrier-pigeon. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Probably our English would be found as docible and ingenious as the Turkish pigeons, which carry letters from Aleppo to Babylon, if trained up accordingly. But such practices by these ''wing-posts'' would spoil many a foot-post. {{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Northamptonshire, II. 498.}}}}
